Chapter: DeskEditDropTest
  cites Works chapter GopDesk
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 When a close drops the Editor's buffer pointer. The pointer names a 9 MB
 block `ged-ensure` reuses whenever it is nonzero, so a pointer left naming
 memory another pane now owns is a write into that pane.

 THE DISCRIMINATING CASE is the first line: the Editor closed while buried,
 so nothing is restored and the target sits above the buffer. The rule before
 this arm kept the pointer there, and the dead span it sat in was a hole any
 heavy open could reuse. The other lines are controls that both rules agree
 on: another pane's buried close keeps it, a restore reaching it drops it, and
 an unset pointer stays unset.

Section: Body

  ded-yn : Boolean -> Text
  ded-yn (v) = if v then "PASS" else "FAIL"

  ded-after : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ded-after (eb) (p) (tgt) (fid) =
    let s = poke-32 eb 0 p
    in let d = desk-edit-drop eb tgt fid
    in peek-32 eb 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let eb = alloc-zeroed 192 16
    in act
      print-line-uni ("the Editor closed while buried drops its pointer: " & ded-yn (ded-after eb 5000 9000 desk-focus-edit == 0))
      print-line-uni ("CONTROL, another pane's buried close keeps it: " & ded-yn (ded-after eb 5000 9000 desk-focus-files == 5000))
      print-line-uni ("CONTROL, a restore reaching the buffer drops it: " & ded-yn (ded-after eb 5000 4000 desk-focus-files == 0))
      print-line-uni ("CONTROL, a restore to the buffer's own address drops it: " & ded-yn (ded-after eb 5000 5000 desk-focus-files == 0))
      print-line-uni ("CONTROL, an unset pointer stays unset: " & ded-yn (ded-after eb 0 9000 desk-focus-edit == 0))
    end
  end